\begin_layout Section
Intro
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this document, I'm going to have another attempt at wrapping my noggin
 around the core-periphery model and related ideas.
 I want to get the intuition nailed, as well as pull out some of the gnarly
 maths problems that continue to niggle at me.
 So it might be in a slightly peculiar order.
 As much as possible I'll try and frame the problems as questions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Where did the externalities go? 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The problem: pecuniary externalities are at the heart of the core model
 - or so the theory goes.
 But where are they? In the simplest of the final equations, much of the
 earlier structure drops away through various arguments.
 The place where I'd expect PEs to come from - increasing returns - is no
 longer there.
 Now, of course, Krugman himself says in the original paper, ``it is possible
 to develop a very simple model of geographical concentration of manufacturing
 based on the interaction of economies of scale with transportation costs.''
 (increts p.484) So where did economies of scale go?
\end_layout

